                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_X
          %h3#in_terms_of_sheaves_on_the_derham_space In terms of sheaves on the deRham space
          %p
            The category of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may equivalently be identified with the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent sheaves
            on its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deRham+space"} deRham space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dR
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dR(X)
            (in non-smooth case one needs to work in derived setting, with de Rham stack instead).
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#LurieCrystal"}> Lurie, above theorem 0.4
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#GaitsgoryRozenblyum11"} Gaitsgory-Rozenblyum 11, 2.1.1
          %p Remembering, from this discussion there, that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the deRham space is the decategorification of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+shape+modality"} infinitesimal path groupoid
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mi inf
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{inf}(X)
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                a quasicoherent sheaf on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dR
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dR(X)
                is a generalization of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
                on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                a vector bundle with a flat
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant
                vector bundle on the infinitesimal path
                = succeed "-groupoid" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Π
                        %mi inf
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi^{inf}
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p this shows pretty manifestly how D-modules are “sheaves of modules with flat connection”, as described more below.
          %h2#meaning_and_usage Meaning and usage
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            \-modules are useful as a means of applying the methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+and+Sheaves"} sheaf theory
            to the study of analytic systems of partial differential equations.
          %p
            Insofar as an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ringed+site"} ringed site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, O)
            can be interpreted as a generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of sections of a vector bundle on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of sections of a vector bundle on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em
              with flat
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection"} connection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            on a section
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            of the sheaf (over some patch
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi v
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \mapsto \nabla_v \sigma
            with respect to the flat connection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
          %p
            In fact when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a complex analytic manifold, any
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_X
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi O
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_X
            = succeed "-modules" do
